---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

// 18 October 2026 (XC): B18 goes on the site as a project introduction only, no manuscript and
// no results. 26 October 2026 (XC): the preprint is on Zenodo (concept DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23677586,
// CC BY 4.0, Vault B18f8); the page links it and otherwise stays a project introduction.
const project = projects.vagueness;
---

<Base
  title={`${project.title} · Xiaochuan Li`}
  description="Speakers disagree about where vague words such as tall end. For a learner that sees only text, is that disagreement noise, or evidence about the scale underneath? Preprint on Zenodo."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research#research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Research & Projects</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">{project.status}</div>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">{project.question}</p>
    <p>{project.summary}</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="/images/research/vagueness-concept-cover.png" width="1260" height="476" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Left: figures ordered by height, with one figure near the tall/average boundary called tall by one speaker and average by others. Right: a listener told tall guesses a little wrong either way, while a learner who reads both sentences learns that average and tall are neighbouring categories." />
      <figcaption>One remark, two readers: the listener pays a small cost, the learner gains evidence about the order of the categories.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p>
      Lipman (2025) showed that when speaker and listener share a goal, vagueness cannot do better than
      precision. That result is about communication. This project adds a learner who sees only text.
      For such a learner, the misdescriptions that vagueness produces are informative: an object that
      some speakers call <em>tall</em> and others call <em>average</em> shows that the two categories
      are neighbours. Near agreement, such a misdescription costs the listener little, because the object
      lies close to a boundary, yet it tells the learner something about the order of the categories.
    </p>
    <p>
      A short proposition states when an objective that gives weight to this learner is not maximised by
      a perfectly precise vocabulary. It does not overturn the result on communication alone.
    </p>

    <h2>Predictions</h2>
    <p>
      Four predictions follow: without disagreement, no order among the categories can be recovered from
      text; a little disagreement is enough; the direction of disagreement carries the order; and
      aggregating speakers by majority vote erases it.
    </p>

    <h2>Learners and data</h2>
    <p>
      The learners are language models trained only on naming text, from small transformers and LSTMs
      trained from scratch to a fine-tuned pretrained model. The tests use an artificial language with a
      known underlying scale, and the colour naming of individual speakers in the World Color Survey.
      Success criteria were fixed before the confirmatory runs, and each confirmed result was
      re-implemented independently from the written specification.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      Preprint, October 2026: Li, X. (2026). <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23677586"><em>What Can Be Learned from Vagueness</em></a>. Zenodo. doi:10.5281/zenodo.23677586 (CC BY 4.0).
    </p>
    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]"><a href="/research">← Back to Research & Projects</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
